"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][22]={"school_physics_en:M22-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M22-L1 – Historical Atomic Models: Observations and Explanations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Atomic models, energy levels and spectra<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why did most alpha particles cross a thin gold foil nearly\nundeflected while a few scattered through large angles?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>atomic model<\/strong> is a testable representation, not a\nphotograph of an atom. <strong>Thomson’s<\/strong> model (around 1904)\nplaced electrons within an extended distribution of positive charge. It\naccommodated negatively charged constituents but could not account for\nthe large deflections of some alpha particles in thin-foil experiments.\nGeiger and Marsden’s scattering results, interpreted by\n<strong>Rutherford<\/strong> (1911), indicated that positive charge and\nnearly all atomic mass are concentrated in a tiny nucleus; the nucleus\noccupies only a small part of the atom’s volume. Rare large-angle\nscattering conflicts with a diffuse positive-charge model.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Bohr’s model<\/strong> (1913) linked discrete electron\nenergies to lines in hydrogen’s spectrum. However, its pictorial\ncircular orbits are not a general quantum description of electrons. The\n<strong>quantum model<\/strong> uses states and spatial probability\ndistributions, not predetermined paths. A later model is not better\nmerely because it is newer: it should account for more evidence through\ntestable assumptions. The national German Abitur core covers qualitative\nquantum atomic models; the historical comparison is a teaching route\ntowards that objective.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_133_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four original panels compare diffuse charge, small nucleus, Bohr orbits and a synthetic quantum probability cloud.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four original panels compare diffuse\ncharge, small nucleus, Bohr orbits and a synthetic quantum probability\ncloud.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Atomic models are explanations, not photographs.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>In a <strong>schematic<\/strong> sample, 9,900 of 10,000 alpha\nparticles reach a detector almost straight ahead, 99 are deflected\nnoticeably and one is backscattered strongly. The fractions are 99%,\n0.99% and 0.01%. These invented teaching counts illustrate frequency\ndifferences; they are not historical Rutherford data and cannot\ndetermine nuclear radius.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Compare four original model sketches, identifying one observation\neach model accounts for and one question it leaves open. Use a\nsimulation or supplied data, not radioactive sources.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which observation challenges a diffuse positive-charge model?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is highly concentrated in Rutherford’s nuclear model?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What hydrogen observation did discrete Bohr energy states\nexplain?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is an electron cloud not a photographic electron\ntrajectory?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The rare large-angle deflections of alpha particles.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Positive charge and almost all of the mass.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Discrete spectral lines and their energy differences.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It illustrates a quantum position probability distribution, not a\nrecorded classical path.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Each model has a limited domain. Bohr’s model does not generally\nexplain many-electron atoms; Rutherford alone does not explain stable\ndiscrete energies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M21&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M21-L6<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L2<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 22, §2.6.3: atomic models;\ncommon core and advanced addition · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/6-4-bohrs-model-of-the-hydrogen-atom\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §6.4 Bohr’s Model<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §6.4: hydrogen energies,\nmodel history and spectral series · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/8-1-the-hydrogen-atom\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §8.1 Hydrogen Atom<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §8.1: hydrogen quantum\nstates and orbitals · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M22-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M22-L2 – Emission and Absorption Spectra<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Atomic models, energy levels and spectra<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why does a hot solid often emit a broad spectrum whereas a thin\nexcited gas shows narrow colour lines?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>spectrum<\/strong> displays light according to wavelength or\nfrequency. A dense hot emitter may produce an approximately\n<strong>continuous spectrum<\/strong>. An excited dilute atomic gas can\nproduce <strong>emission lines<\/strong> when atoms release photons of\nselected energies. When broad-spectrum light crosses a suitable cooler\ngas, <strong>absorption lines<\/strong> may appear at transition\nfrequencies in the transmitted light. Their visibility also depends on\ntemperature, level populations, gas column density and detector\nresolution.<\/p>\n<p>Each line satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E=hf=hc/\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe same pair of energy levels can produce absorption or emission,\ndepending on the transition direction. Spectrometers do not show\ninfinitely narrow lines: instrumental resolution, line broadening and\noverlap matter. Hydrogen’s Balmer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nline in air is near\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>656.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">656.3\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand appears red. A material is better identified through a\n<strong>pattern of multiple lines<\/strong> rather than a single matching\ncolour.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_134_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic coloured bands showing a continuum, emission lines and absorption gaps.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic coloured bands showing a\ncontinuum, emission lines and absorption gaps.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Emission and absorption lines relate to discrete atomic\ntransitions.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>For a red line at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>656<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=656\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1240<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><mn>656<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.89<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{ph}}\\approx1240\\,\\mathrm{eV\\,nm}/656\\,\\mathrm{nm}=1.89\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAbsorption in the reverse transition would use the same photon energy;\nactual linewidths and transition probabilities are ignored here.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Draw three original schematic spectra and match them to a broadband\nemitter, an excited atomic gas, and broadband light passing through an\nabsorbing gas. Use supplied spectral data or a supervised enclosed\nlow-voltage classroom instrument.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What distinguishes an emission line spectrum?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why might absorption occur at an emission line wavelength?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What photon energy approximately corresponds to 620 nm?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a single red line often insufficient to identify an\nelement?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Bright discrete lines on a darker background.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The same energy gap is involved, but absorption is the upward\ntransition.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1240<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>620<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1240/620\\approx2.00\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Several materials or measurement situations may produce similar\nlines; the complete pattern is more informative.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>A schematic dark line does not mean complete removal of all photons\nat that wavelength. Gas conditions, optical depth and instrumental\nresolution determine its appearance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L1<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L3<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 22, §2.6.3: atomic models;\ncommon core and advanced addition · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/8-5-atomic-spectra-and-x-rays\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §8.5 Atomic Spectra<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §8.5: lines, spectral\nanalysis and energy levels · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/pml/atomic-spectra-database\">NIST –\nAtomic Spectra Database<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Reference database: atomic line\nwavelengths and energy levels · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M22-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M22-L3 – Hydrogen Energy Levels and Spectral Lines<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Atomic models, energy levels and spectra<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>How can a transition between two unseen electronic states produce an\nobservable red spectral line?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>An isolated <strong>hydrogen atom<\/strong> has discrete bound states.\nIn a simplified nonrelativistic model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>13.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_n=-13.6\\,\\mathrm{eV}/n^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1,2,\\ldots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand energy zero corresponding to an ionized atom with a distant electron\nat rest. The ground state lies at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>13.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-13.6\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nhigher levels approach\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>During a transition from a higher to a lower state,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_{\\mathrm{photon}}=E_{\\mathrm{upper}}-E_{\\mathrm{lower}}&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Absorption<\/strong> requires a photon with the corresponding\nenergy. The wavelength follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=hc/E_{\\mathrm{photon}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe <strong>Balmer series<\/strong> terminates at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the <strong>Lyman series<\/strong> at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthese occur in different spectral ranges. Energy levels are not physical\nheights above the nucleus: the vertical axis denotes energy. Fine\nstructure, the Lamb shift and air-versus-vacuum wavelength conversion\nare neglected in this school model.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_135_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four hydrogen energy levels with two downward transitions.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four hydrogen energy levels with two\ndownward transitions.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Transitions release specific photon energies; energy spacings\nschematic.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>→<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=3\\to2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>13.6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.511<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_3=-13.6/9=-1.511\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_2=-3.40\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.889<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta E=1.889\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1240<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1.889<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>656.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\approx1240/1.889\\approx656.4\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe simplified calculation approximately explains the red\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nline; high-precision spectroscopy requires corrections.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Plot\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1,2,3,4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst a true energy scale. Mark three transitions and determine which\nreleases the most photon energy. Check the arithmetic in a\nspreadsheet.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the ground-state ionization energy in this model?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the photon energy for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>→<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2\\to1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>→<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=2\\to3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nemission or absorption?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is an energy-level diagram not a scaled electron orbit\ndrawing?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>13.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">13.6\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto the continuum at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3.40<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>13.6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10.2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(-3.40)-(-13.6)=10.2\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\napproximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>121.6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">121.6\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Absorption of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.889<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_3-E_2\\approx1.889\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The spacings represent energies rather than geometric electron\ndistances.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The formula describes an idealised one-electron hydrogen model (and\nhydrogen-like ions with suitable adjustments), not arbitrary neutral\nmany-electron atoms.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L2<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L4<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 22, §2.6.3: atomic models;\ncommon core and advanced addition · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/6-4-bohrs-model-of-the-hydrogen-atom\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §6.4 Bohr’s Model<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §6.4: hydrogen energies,\nmodel history and spectral series · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/pml/atomic-spectra-database\">NIST –\nAtomic Spectra Database<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Reference database: atomic line\nwavelengths and energy levels · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M22-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M22-L4 – Quantum Atomic Model and Orbitals<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Atomic models, energy levels and spectra<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why can electrons in atoms not be pictured as planets following fixed\ntracks?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>In the <strong>quantum atomic model<\/strong>, an electron is\ndescribed by a quantum state. The <strong>wavefunction<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi(\\mathbf r)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay be complex; its squared magnitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi(\\mathbf r)|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a <strong>spatial probability density<\/strong>. Integrating over a\nvolume yields a probability:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(V)=\\int_V|\\psi|^2\\,\\mathrm dV<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNormalization over all space gives one.<\/p>\n<p>An <strong>orbital<\/strong> represents a spatial one-electron state,\nnot the path travelled by an electron. The\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\norbital has spherical density symmetry; suitable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\norbitals have two preferred spatial lobes and a nodal plane. Coloured\norbital surfaces usually depict chosen <strong>density\nisosurfaces<\/strong>, not physical walls. The probability is not uniform\nwithin the pictured outline. The KMK common Abitur core requires a\n<strong>qualitative<\/strong> quantum atomic model, not full\nmultidimensional Schrödinger solutions.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_136_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Synthetic point clouds show spherically symmetric 1s and directional p-orbital illustrations.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Synthetic point clouds show spherically\nsymmetric 1s and directional p-orbital illustrations.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Dot clouds indicate probability densities, not classical electron\ntracks.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>In an artificial <strong>one-dimensional<\/strong> probability\nexample, let the position density be a constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.25\\,\\mathrm{nm}^{-1}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover an interval\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.0\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlong. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P=0.25\\,\\mathrm{nm}^{-1}\\times2.0\\,\\mathrm{nm}=0.50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is <strong>not<\/strong> a real hydrogen orbital and does not\nrepresent three-dimensional atomic density.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Sketch a spherical density and a directional orbital as simplified\ncontours. Label what each outline represents and what it does not\nrepresent. Contrast them with a circular-orbit drawing.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmean in three-dimensional space?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must density be integrated over a region to obtain\nprobability?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What characterizes a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\norbital?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does the outer contour of an orbital graphic usually mean?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Probability density per unit volume.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A density is not yet a dimensionless probability.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Spherical symmetry of the spatial density.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A selected density threshold or probability region, not a hard\natomic boundary.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>These sketches simplify one-electron states. Many-electron states,\ninteractions and electron spin require additional concepts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L3<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L5<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 22, §2.6.3: atomic models;\ncommon core and advanced addition · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/8-1-the-hydrogen-atom\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §8.1 Hydrogen Atom<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §8.1: hydrogen quantum\nstates and orbitals · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/7-1-wave-functions\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §7.1 Wave Functions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §7.1: probability\ndensity and normalization · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M22-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M22-L5 – Advanced Level: One-Dimensional Potential Well<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Atomic models, energy levels and spectra<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Can an electron confined to a region of finite width have any\narbitrarily small energy?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>The <strong>one-dimensional infinite potential well<\/strong> is an\nideal model:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U(x)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;x&lt;L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand the potential is infinite outside. The wavefunction vanishes at the\nwalls:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi(0)=\\psi(L)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nInside the well, stationary states obey the time-independent Schrödinger\nequation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mi>ψ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-(\\hbar^2/2m)\\psi&#39;&#39;=E\\psi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe boundary conditions permit only standing sine waves with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_n=n\\pi/L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1,2,\\dots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>The normalized functions are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi_n(x)=\\sqrt{2/L}\\,\\sin(n\\pi x/L)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the <strong>discrete energies<\/strong> are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_n=n^2\\pi^2\\hbar^2/(2mL^2)=n^2h^2/(8mL^2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn particular,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_1&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nthe ground-state energy cannot vanish because a confined normalized\nstate cannot be spatially constant and zero at both walls. State\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninterior nodes. In the German national KMK standards, this is explicitly\nan <strong>additional advanced-level topic<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_137_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three standing sine states for n=1 to 3 between fixed walls.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three standing sine states for n=1 to 3\nbetween fixed walls.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Boundary conditions quantize the allowed states and\nenergies.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>For an electron of mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.109<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>31<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_e=9.109\\times10^{-31}\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin a width\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=1.00\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.376<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_1\\approx0.376\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.504<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_2=4E_1\\approx1.504\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.128<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_2-E_1\\approx1.128\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDoubling\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreduces each energy at fixed quantum number by a factor of four.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Draw the stationary sine functions for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1,2,3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith zeros at both walls. Sketch\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>ψ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\psi_n|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand avoid treating the waves as classical particles rebounding from\nwalls.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How do the boundary conditions select discrete\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_3/E_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the box not a complete real atomic model?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Only integer numbers of half-wavelengths fit:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_n=n\\pi/L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_3/E_1=9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It falls to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_1/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhere approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.094<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.094\\,\\mathrm{eV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It is one-dimensional and assumes infinitely high boundaries rather\nthan a real Coulomb potential.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Infinite walls are a mathematical idealisation; actual quantum\nsystems may tunnel through finite barriers. A box is not the Coulomb\nfield of an atom.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L4<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L6<\/a> · Next lesson<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 22, §2.6.3: atomic models;\ncommon core and advanced addition · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/7-4-the-quantum-particle-in-a-box\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §7.4 Particle in a Box<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §7.4: stationary\nstates and discrete box energies · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/7-1-wave-functions\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §7.1 Wave Functions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §7.1: probability\ndensity and normalization · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M22-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M22-L6 – Atomic Physics in Research and Technology: Spectral Analysis<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M22 – Atomic models, energy levels and spectra<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>How can a spectrum help identify an unknown gas without directly\nexamining it?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Atomic spectroscopy<\/strong> links measured wavelengths to\ntransitions between atomic states. A basic spectrometer disperses light\nthrough a grating or prism and records an intensity profile\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I(\\lambda)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConverting detector pixels into physical wavelengths requires\n<strong>calibration points<\/strong> or an instrument wavelength\ncalibration. Only then can line centres, uncertainties and instrumental\nresolution be compared with reference data. Reliable\n<strong>identification<\/strong> normally uses multiple compatible lines\nand checks for interference.<\/p>\n<p>Astronomical spectra also require assumptions about light\npropagation, temperature and ionization state before drawing\ncompositional conclusions. <strong>NIST<\/strong> provides critically\nevaluated line and energy-level datasets; consulting such a database is\nnot the same as producing a gas discharge spectrum in a laboratory.\nOther applications include materials inspection and gas analysis. In the\nKMK common core, this is an application context for emission, absorption\nand energy levels, not a separate nationally compulsory procedure.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_138_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"A pixel axis with calibration marks and synthetic line profiles including a 656 nm marker.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A pixel axis with calibration marks and\nsynthetic line profiles including a 656 nm marker.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>A measured position needs calibration before physical\ninterpretation.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example<\/h2>\n<p>In an invented <strong>linear<\/strong> teaching calibration,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">600\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncorresponds to pixel\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>700<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">700\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=300<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda(x)=600\\,\\mathrm{nm}+0.50\\,\\mathrm{nm/pixel}\\,(x-100)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA line centre at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>212<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=212<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>656<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=656\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is close to the hydrogen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nline but one line alone is not conclusive evidence of composition.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Investigation and practice<\/h2>\n<p>Analyse a supplied example intensity profile. Find two line centres,\ncalculate wavelengths and mark uncertainty intervals. Compare with an\nNIST reference and record possible alternative assignments. Do not build\nor operate unshielded discharge or UV sources.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must a pixel spectrum be calibrated?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What wavelength does the example give at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>180<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=180<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is it useful to compare several spectral lines?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What must be checked when two lines overlap strongly?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calibration supplies the physical wavelength scale.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>600<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>180<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>640<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">600+0.50\\times(180-100)=640\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It reduces the chance of false identification.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Resolution, linewidth, blending and possible alternative\nmodels.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model limitations<\/h2>\n<p>This pixel calibration is artificial and linear; real instruments may\nshow nonlinear dispersion, drift and stray light. The diagram does not\ncontain measurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Further reading (internal)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M22&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M22-L5<\/a> · Review\nprerequisite <strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The next module begins with\n<a href=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L1 – Astrophysics: Stars and\nthe Hertzsprung–Russell Diagram<\/a>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S8\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.kmk.org/fileadmin/Dateien/veroeffentlichungen_beschluesse/2020/2020_06_18-BildungsstandardsAHR_Physik.pdf\">KMK\n– Physics standards for Abitur 2020<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · p. 22, §2.6.3: atomic models;\ncommon core and advanced addition · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/8-5-atomic-spectra-and-x-rays\">OpenStax\n– University Physics 3, §8.5 Atomic Spectra<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §8.5: lines, spectral\nanalysis and energy levels · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.nist.gov/pml/atomic-spectra-database\">NIST –\nAtomic Spectra Database<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Reference database: atomic line\nwavelengths and energy levels · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2 id=\"S9\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Evidence<\/h2>\n<\/article>"};
